    "result_text": "All 30 tests pass, including the new `Cp19Tests`. The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit rule following the existing quota-rule pattern in the codebase (the same shape as `CityAtCapacityException` / `rejectCityAtCapacity`):\n\n1. **`DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending date, in the `rest.controller` package alongside the other rule exceptions.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `rejectDailyLimit()` call to the create pipeline. The new `rejectDailyLimit()` method counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals the server's current date and throws when that count is `>= 100` (`DAILY_LIMIT`). The count is taken before the new owner is persisted.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` for the new exception that returns **429 Too Many Requests** with a `ProblemDetail` body, consistent with the other handlers (including an `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]` property).\n\nNote the placement: `rejectDailyLimit()` runs after `defaultRegistrationDate` isn't strictly required since the count is based on `LocalDate.now()` against existing owners' registration dates, matching the spec's \"created today (by registrationDate)\". The test seeds 100 owners (all defaulted to today), and the 101st is correctly rejected with 429.",
